  "root_cause": "The dedicated aiat-wsl daemon was the correct daemon. On it, mas_internal had valid bridge/IPAM/endpoints, service DNS, container routes, and ARP state, but inter-container TCP probes timed out. Recreating only the AIAT Compose bridge through the canonical wrapper (mas.sh down without -v, then normal up) restored east-west TCP; all nine persistent volumes remained. Docker Desktop default is a separate daemon and caused published host-port collisions when its default Compose ports were selected, not the original bridge outage. The privileged bridge firewall/forwarding layer could not be inspected, so the lower-level mechanism is unconfirmed.",
  "schema_version": "aiat.local-compose-connectivity-evidence.v1",
  "scope": "local WSL development Compose only; not native-Linux certification",
  "selected_context": "aiat-wsl",
  "selected_context_note": "AIAT Compose containers run on the dedicated WSL daemon; Docker Desktop default is a distinct daemon and data root. That distinction caused host-port collisions with the wrong published-port profile; it did not explain the original inter-container timeout, which cleared when the AIAT bridge was recreated.",
